# Presence checks & readable control flow

## Presence / truthiness checks — never `typeof`

Do not use `typeof` checks. Prefer presence / duck-typing (`value`, `value?.trim`, `value?.split`, `Set.has`). Trust callers and TypeScript types; coerce lightly at boundaries. Only validate real invariants (required fields, range bounds).

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — typeof noise
if (typeof value === "string" && value.trim()) {
  return value.trim();
}

if (typeof value === "object") { /* ... */ }

.filter((o) => typeof o === "string")

// ✅ GOOD — presence / duck-typing
if (candidate?.trim?.()) {
  return candidate.trim();
}

if (value) {
  const { chapter, verse } = value as QuizVerseRef;
}

.map((o) => (o as string)?.trim?.()).filter(Boolean)
```

For string vs object shapes, try the object fields first; fall back to `` `${value}`.split(...) `` — do not branch on `value.split` (functions are always truthy when typed as string).

Do not add per-param typeof helpers (`asDifficulty`, `asFocus`, etc.) when a shared `pick` / default is enough. Keep snake_case aliases only at the request boundary.

## No one-line returns / throws

Always use braces. Put `return` / `throw` on their own line so the branch is easy to scan.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
if (c && v) return { chapter: c, verse: v };
if (!bookName) throw new QuizGenerateError("book is required");

// ✅ GOOD
if (c && v) {
  return { chapter: c, verse: v };
}

if (!bookName) {
  throw new QuizGenerateError("book is required");
}
```
